**Document — Letter to a local newspaper (invented)**

> Dear Editor,
> Last week my fourteen-year-old son brought home a novel from the school library. It describes violence and bad language on almost every page. I do not want my taxes to pay for books that corrupt children. Parents have a right to protect their families. This novel must be removed from the library at once.
> — A worried father

**Task.** Write a reply to the editor (about 150 words, in English). You must:
1. **identify the two camps** and what each one speaks in the name of;
2. **distinguish** what a book shows from what it is accused of;
3. use at least **two arguments** from the debate studied in class and **one historical example** of a trial;
4. **conclude with nuance**.

**Document — Extract from a dystopian short story (invented)**

> They took my name on the first day and gave me a number: 4-11. Numbers do not need to be loved, the Warden says. Every morning the screen in my room asks how I slept, and every morning I answer “Very well, thank you”, because the screen knows when I lie.
> We are not allowed to read. But at night I recite the names of the streets of my old town, one by one, in the dark. Mill Lane. Chapel Road. Rose Street. As long as I remember them, the town still exists, and so do I.

**Task.** In about 150 words:
1. identify the **real starting point** that is pushed to the extreme;
2. study the **power**: surveillance, language, identity;
3. show how the narrator **resists**;
4. compare with **Offred** in *The Handmaid’s Tale* on one precise point.
